# guessing.zd — a bounded disclosure, which is §19's whole subject.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `release` is the construct the confidentiality
# story rests on, and until this landed no program in `examples/` declared
# one. That is not a gap in coverage the way a missing unit test is: every
# gate this repository points at the corpus — `resolve_examples.rs`,
# `codegen_diagnostics.rs`, `fmt_examples.rs`, the browser sweep — was
# reading a corpus with no `release` in it, so none of them had ever seen
# one emitted. The emitter dropped the body and shipped a handler that
# called a function it never defined; the manual's own §19 example did it,
# and nothing failed.
#
# WHAT THE PROGRAM IS. The answer is `durable`, so it lives on the server
# and the browser never receives it. A guess is `client`. `judge` compares
# the two *on the server* and returns one bit — did they match — which is
# the disclosure, and `limit 20 per visitor` is the bound on it.
#
# WHY THE RESULT IS AN `Option`. A budgeted release returns `Option of T`
# so that running out cannot be forgotten: §19.2 rule 5 makes exhaustion a
# case the view has to write, rather than a silent `no`.
state answer is durable Text            starting "cabbage"
state guess  is client  Text            starting ""
state result is server  Option of Truth from judge with guess, answer

release judge with guess, answer
    gives Truth
    trusted guess
    trusted answer
    limit 20 per visitor
    give guess is answer

view
    Column
        Heading "Guess the word"

        Input guess, hint is "your guess"

        # Three arms because the answer crosses a boundary, and a fourth
        # nested one because a budget can run out. Neither is optional:
        # `Remote of T` and `Option of T` are both closed choices.
        when result
            Loading show Text "thinking…"
            Failed with error show Text "the judge is unavailable"
            Ready with verdict
                when verdict
                    None
                        Text "no guesses left"
                    Some with right
                        if right
                            Text "yes, that is the word"
                        otherwise
                            Text "no, try again"
